# Run `mise run setup`, which creates .env when there is none and writes the
# ten values it can fill in on its own. Copy what else you need from this
# file into .env by hand. mise loads .env for every task it runs.
#
# Secrets, per-install identifiers and the knobs worth turning without an edit
# live here. Everything else is a settings file: config/settings/default.yml
# holds what is the same in every environment, and development.yml, test.yml
# and production.yml hold what is not. A value set in one of those wins over
# the variable of the same name, which is why site.url has no variable below.
#
# A variable set to nothing is not the same as a variable left out, so the
# lines below are either a real value or a comment.

# Required to sign in to the admin. Register a GitHub OAuth app whose callback
# URL is <site url>/auth/github/callback and paste its two halves here. A
# laptop and the live site each need their own app, so these are the two
# values that change between them. Without both, the admin refuses every
# sign in.
GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=
GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Optional, and the first thing to change if you fork this. The GitHub account
# the admin lets in: sign in as anybody else and the admin turns you away. Read
# yours from https://api.github.com/users/<your login>. It defaults to mine.
# OWNER_GITHUB_ID=931094

# Required to run anything, and all ten written by
# `mise run setup:environment`. `mise run dev` and `mise run test` start
# Postgres and Redis from .config/compose.yml, which reads the same variables,
# so the app and the containers agree, and the two users are the ones the
# containers create. `mise run dev` also starts RustFS, whose root key is the
# MEDIA_STORE pair, and makes the blog-development bucket in it.
# ANALYTICS_SALT, APP_SECRET and READER_SALT are each at least 64 characters,
# and the app refuses to load when any two match: ANALYTICS_SALT goes into the
# daily visitor hash, READER_SALT into each post's reader hash, APP_SECRET
# signs the admin session, and one leak must not undo another. Rotate
# ANALYTICS_SALT whenever you like, which starts the visitor hashes over
# without signing the admin out. Leave READER_SALT alone: a new one counts
# every reader of a post again. Production is the only environment that reads
# any of the three: development and test pin their own in
# config/settings/<env>.yml.
# ANALYTICS_SALT=
# APP_SECRET=
# COMPOSE_FILE=./.config/compose.yml
# DATABASE_PASSWORD=
# DATABASE_USER=postgres
# MEDIA_STORE_ACCESS_KEY=blog
# MEDIA_STORE_SECRET_KEY=
# READER_SALT=
# REDIS_PASSWORD=
# REDIS_USER=redis

# Optional. The token the commit import, the project refresh and the issue
# sync read GitHub with. Make a classic personal access token with the repo
# and read:org scopes, then use Configure SSO beside it on
# https://github.com/settings/tokens to authorize it for each organization that
# enforces SAML single sign-on. An org the token isn't authorized for hides its
# issues from the sync. Without a token none of the three run.
# GITHUB_API_TOKEN=

# Optional. The personal API keys the issue sync reads Linear with, comma
# separated. A key reads only the workspace it was made in, so each workspace
# needs its own: make one under Settings, Account, Security & access in that
# workspace, then add it to the list. Without a key the Linear sync does not
# run.
# LINEAR_API_KEYS=

# Optional. The project key that sends unhandled errors to Honeybadger, from
# the web process and the worker alike. Without it nothing is sent anywhere.
# Production reports and the other environments do not. Set
# HONEYBADGER_REPORT_DATA to true or false to override that, which is how you
# watch a report arrive from a laptop. HONEYBADGER_PROJECT_URL is the project's
# page on Honeybadger, which the admin security page links to.
# HONEYBADGER_API_KEY=
# HONEYBADGER_PROJECT_URL=
# HONEYBADGER_REPORT_DATA=

# Optional. Without them the site posts to no network. Bluesky needs an app
# password for the handle in config/settings/default.yml, Mastodon an access
# token for the instance there.
# BLUESKY_APP_PASSWORD=
# MASTODON_ACCESS_TOKEN=

# Optional, and needed together. They download the GeoLite2 City database
# that names the city and country behind an address. Without both, visits are
# stored with no country.
# MAXMIND_ACCOUNT_ID=
# MAXMIND_LICENSE_KEY=

# Optional, and read in production only: development points at the RustFS
# that `mise run dev` starts, and test reaches no store. Where photos live,
# in any S3-compatible store. Production reads the MEDIA_STORE pair above as
# the key to the bucket. Without the bucket and both halves of the key the
# site takes no uploads. Leave MEDIA_STORE_ENDPOINT out for AWS itself. Set
# MEDIA_STORE_PATH_STYLE to true for a store that wants the bucket in the
# path rather than the host name, as RustFS does. docs/media-store.md walks
# through the bucket and a key that reaches only that bucket.
# MEDIA_STORE_BUCKET=
# MEDIA_STORE_ENDPOINT=
# MEDIA_STORE_PATH_STYLE=false
# MEDIA_STORE_REGION=us-east-1

# Optional, and read in production only. Where the nightly database dumps go,
# in any S3-compatible store, with a key of their own apart from the
# MEDIA_STORE one. Without the bucket and both halves of the key the job
# dumps nothing and logs that backups are not configured. docs/backups.md
# walks through the bucket, a key that reaches only that bucket, and the
# pg_dump client the worker needs.
# BACKUP_STORE_ACCESS_KEY=
# BACKUP_STORE_BUCKET=
# BACKUP_STORE_ENDPOINT=
# BACKUP_STORE_PATH_STYLE=false
# BACKUP_STORE_REGION=us-east-1
# BACKUP_STORE_SECRET_KEY=

# Optional. Names the database the app and every `db:` task act on, so set it
# to work in a second database without touching the first. Development and
# production use the name as it stands and test adds _test to it, so the suite
# cannot empty the database development serves. Unset, development uses
# blog_development, test blog_test and production blog. `HANAMI_ENV` picks the
# environment, and each task acts on that one database and no other.
# `mise run setup:databases` prepares the two local ones.
# DATABASE_NAME=

# Optional, with defaults. Where Postgres, Redis and the local RustFS answer.
# DATABASE_HOST and REDIS_HOST are read in production only; development and
# test use localhost. MEDIA_STORE_PORT is read in development only.
# DATABASE_HOST=localhost
# DATABASE_MAX_CONNECTIONS=12
# DATABASE_PORT=5432
# MEDIA_STORE_PORT=9000
# REDIS_HOST=localhost
# REDIS_PORT=6379

# Optional. How long Sidekiq waits on Redis, in seconds, and how it tries again
# after a dropped connection. REDIS_TIMEOUT covers connect, read and write, and
# REDIS_CONNECT_TIMEOUT overrides it for connect alone. REDIS_RECONNECT_ATTEMPTS
# takes a whole number of tries, made at once, or a comma separated list of
# seconds to wait before each try: 0.5,1,2 tries three times, after waits of
# half a second, one second and two seconds. Unset, Sidekiq keeps its own
# defaults: a 3 second timeout and one retry.
# REDIS_CONNECT_TIMEOUT=
# REDIS_RECONNECT_ATTEMPTS=
# REDIS_TIMEOUT=

# Optional, and read in production only, where config/settings/production.yml
# names CF-Connecting-IP as the header the tunnel writes. The addresses and
# CIDR ranges, comma separated, whose requests may carry that header. A
# request from anywhere else counts under the address it connected from,
# whatever the header says. The default trusts the loopback, where cloudflared
# connects from. Set it to nothing to trust no one.
# PROXY_TRUSTED_PROXIES=127.0.0.0/8,::1/128

# Optional, with defaults. How the web server runs. Puma listens on the
# loopback, where cloudflared reaches it, so no one else on the network can
# get around the tunnel. Set HANAMI_HOST to 0.0.0.0 to listen on every
# interface. HANAMI_MIN_THREADS falls back to HANAMI_MAX_THREADS, so the pool
# is one size unless you split it.
# HANAMI_ENV=development
# HANAMI_HOST=127.0.0.1
# HANAMI_MAX_THREADS=5
# HANAMI_MIN_THREADS=
# HANAMI_PORT=2300
# HANAMI_WEB_CONCURRENCY=0

# Optional, with defaults. How many requests each of the four throttles takes
# from one visitor, and over how many minutes. config/settings.rb holds the
# numbers the app falls back to, and a variable here wins over them. A window
# must be shorter than the day a visitor hash lasts, and a limit of nothing
# refuses the first request, so both are refused at boot.
# ANALYTICS_THROTTLE_LIMIT=120
# ANALYTICS_THROTTLE_WINDOW_MINUTES=60
# CLIENT_REGISTRATION_THROTTLE_LIMIT=10
# CLIENT_REGISTRATION_THROTTLE_WINDOW_MINUTES=60
# CONTACT_THROTTLE_LIMIT=3
# CONTACT_THROTTLE_WINDOW_MINUTES=60
# WEBMENTIONS_THROTTLE_LIMIT=30
# WEBMENTIONS_THROTTLE_WINDOW_MINUTES=60

# Optional, with a default. How many webmentions the site takes from all
# senders together over the webmention window, so many addresses cannot fill
# the queue of pages to fetch.
# WEBMENTIONS_TOTAL_THROTTLE_LIMIT=100

# Optional, with a default. How many messages the contact form takes from all
# senders together over the contact window, so many addresses cannot flood the
# inbox. Messages filed as spam count toward it.
# CONTACT_TOTAL_THROTTLE_LIMIT=20

# Optional, with defaults. How many seconds the contact form must sit open
# before a send counts, and how many hours its stamp lasts. A send made sooner,
# later or with no valid stamp stores nothing and reads as sent.
# CONTACT_MINIMUM_SUBMIT_SECONDS=3
# CONTACT_STAMP_EXPIRY_HOURS=24

# Optional, with a default. How many MCP clients may register from all
# senders together over the registration window, so many addresses cannot
# fill the clients table.
# CLIENT_REGISTRATION_TOTAL_THROTTLE_LIMIT=30
